# Line endings are pinned, not left to each clone's core.autocrlf.
#
# This project publishes a SHA256 of the compiled script and tells people to
# check it. That promise only holds if building from a fresh clone reproduces
# the same bytes - and it does not if one machine checks out LF and another
# CRLF, because build.ps1 concatenates these files verbatim. Every clone gets
# CRLF, which is what Windows PowerShell sources use anyway.
* text=auto eol=crlf

# PowerShell sources are text, stated rather than detected.
#
# text=auto leaves it to a heuristic, and the heuristic gets it wrong: git
# classifies test\Invoke-DryRunHarness.ps1 as -text - no NUL bytes, no long
# lines, nothing non-printable, and every comparable file in the tree detected
# correctly. A file it calls binary is never normalised, so it commits with
# whatever line endings the working copy had and every later diff is the whole
# file.
#
# That is a nuisance in a test and a hazard in src\: build.ps1 concatenates
# those files verbatim, and the published fingerprint is only reproducible from
# a clone if every one of them checks out with the same bytes everywhere. That
# is too much to leave to detection.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf

# Genuinely binary, never touched. Listed explicitly rather than left to
# text=auto's detection: an image that gets line-ending conversion applied to it
# is corrupt, and it is corrupt in the clone rather than here, which is the
# worst place to find out.
*.png  binary
*.webp binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.zip  binary
*.nip  binary
